Convergent evolution is a process where different species independently evolve similar traits or characteristics in response to similar environmental pressures. This can result in species that are not closely related having similar adaptations. 
A rare example of convergent evolution is the similarities between the wings of bats and birds. Bats are mammals, while birds are reptiles, yet both have evolved wings with similar structures to enable flight. 
Parallel evolution, on the other hand, refers to the process where similar traits evolve in related species due to shared ancestry. 
One practical application of convergent evolution is in the development of biomimetic technologies. By studying the adaptations of different species, engineers can design technologies that mimic these adaptations to solve human problems. For example, studying the hydrodynamic properties of shark skin has led to the development of more efficient swimsuits for athletes. 
