    if err != nil {
        panic(err)
    }
    // Split the dataset into training and testing sets
    trainData, testData := base.InstancesTrainTestSplit(dataset, 0.7)
    // Create a random forest classifier
    rf := ensemble.NewRandomForest(10, 3)
    // Train the random forest classifier
    rf.Fit(trainData)
    // Make predictions on the test set using the random forest classifier
    rfPreds, err := rf.Predict(testData)
    if err != nil {
        panic(err)
    }
    // Evaluate the performance of the random forest classifier
    rfAcc := evaluation.GetAccuracy(testData, rfPreds)
    fmt.Printf("Random Forest Accuracy: %.2f%%\n", rfAcc*100)
    // Create a gradient boosting classifier
    gb := ensemble.NewGradientBoosting(10, 0.1, 1.0, trees.NewID3DecisionTree())
    // Train the gradient boosting classifier
    gb.Fit(trainData)
    // Make predictions on the test set using the gradient boosting classifier
    gbPreds, err := gb.Predict(testData)
    if err != nil {
        panic(err)
    }
    // Evaluate the performance of the gradient boosting classifier
    gbAcc := evaluation.GetAccuracy(testData, gbPreds)
    fmt.Printf("Gradient Boosting Accuracy: %.2f%%\n", gbAcc*100)
}
```
In this example, we load a dataset and split it into training and testing sets. We then create a random forest classifier and a gradient boosting classifier. We train both classifiers on the training set and make predictions on the test set using each classifier. Finally, we evaluate the performance of each classifier using accuracy.
If the dataset is imbalanced, gradient boosting may be more effective than random forest because gradient boosting can focus more on the minority class. Gradient boosting works by iteratively adding weak learners to the ensemble, with each learner focusing on the samples that were misclassified by the previous learners. This means that the gradient boosting classifier can learn to prioritize the minority class and improve its accuracy on those samples.
In contrast, random forest works by building multiple decision trees on random subsets of the data and combining their predictions. While random forest can also handle imbalanced datasets, it may not be as effective as gradient boosting in prioritizing the minority class.
So, in summary, if the dataset is imbalanced and the goal is to prioritize the minority class, gradient boosting may be more effective than random forest.

