Here is a MATLAB code that generates truth tables for any given boolean expression:
```
% Define the boolean expression
expression = '(A & ~B) | (~A & B)';
% Define the variables used in the expression
variables = {'A', 'B'};
% Calculate the number of rows in the truth table
num_rows = 2^length(variables);
% Generate the truth table
truth_table = zeros(num_rows, length(variables) + 1);
for i = 1:num_rows
    % Convert the row number to a binary string
    binary_string = dec2bin(i-1, length(variables));

    % Convert the binary string to a row of 0's and 1's
    row = str2num(binary_string(:))';

    % Evaluate the expression for the current row
    truth_table(i, 1:length(variables)) = row;
    truth_table(i, end) = eval(expression);
end
% Display the truth table
disp('Truth Table:');
disp([variables, 'Output']);
disp(truth_table);
```
To use this code, simply replace the `expression` variable with your own boolean expression and the `variables` variable with the variables used in the expression. The code will output a truth table with the variables and the output of the expression for each row.

