How can we implement Quick Sort algorithm in JavaScript to sort an array of integers in ascending order? Please provide the JavaScript code for this algorithm and explain its time and space complexity. Also, can you discuss its advantages and disadvantages over other sorting algorithms like Merge Sort and Bubble Sort? 
Here is the JavaScript code for Quick Sort algorithm: 
function quickSort(arr) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) {
    return arr;
  }

  const pivot = arr[arr.length - 1];
  const leftArr = [];
  const rightArr = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
    if (arr[i] < pivot) {
      leftArr.push(arr[i]);
    } else {
      rightArr.push(arr[i]);
    }
  }

  return [...quickSort(leftArr), pivot, ...quickSort(rightArr)];
}
The time complexity of Quick Sort is O(n log n) in average case and O(n^2) in worst case. The space complexity is O(log n) in average case and O(n) in worst case.
One advantage of Quick Sort over Merge Sort is that it has a smaller constant factor in the time complexity, making it faster for small arrays. One disadvantage of Quick Sort is that it has worst case time complexity of O(n^2), which can happen when the pivot is chosen poorly. In contrast, Merge Sort has a worst case time complexity of O(n log n) regardless of the input. Bubble Sort is much slower than both Quick Sort and Merge Sort, with a worst case time complexity of O(n^2).

