\section{Introduction}
Manual pour-over brewing is a simple and effective way to make a delicious cup of coffee. This method involves pouring hot water over freshly ground coffee beans, allowing the water to extract the coffee's flavor and aroma. This guide will provide step-by-step instructions for brewing coffee using a manual pour-over method, while also taking into account the altitude and temperature of the brewing location.
\section{Equipment}
To brew coffee using a manual pour-over method, you will need the following equipment:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item Pour-over coffee maker (such as a Hario V60 or Chemex)
    \item Paper coffee filter
    \item Freshly roasted coffee beans
    \item Coffee grinder
    \item Digital kitchen scale
    \item Kettle
    \item Timer
    \item Mug or carafe
\end{itemize}
\section{Instructions}
\subsection{Step 1: Boil the Water}
Fill your kettle with fresh, cold water and bring it to a boil. If you are brewing at high altitude, the boiling point of water is lower, so you will need to adjust the temperature accordingly. As a general rule, for every 1,000 feet above sea level, you should decrease the temperature of the water by 2 degrees Fahrenheit. For example, if you are brewing at 5,000 feet above sea level, you should use water that is 10 degrees Fahrenheit cooler than if you were brewing at sea level.
\subsection{Step 2: Grind the Coffee}
Grind your coffee beans to a medium-fine consistency. The amount of coffee you use will depend on your personal taste preferences, but a good starting point is a ratio of 1:16, which means 1 gram of coffee for every 16 grams of water. Use your digital kitchen scale to measure the coffee and water accurately.
\subsection{Step 3: Prepare the Filter}
Fold the paper coffee filter along the seams and place it in the pour-over coffee maker. Rinse the filter with hot water to remove any papery taste and to preheat the coffee maker and mug or carafe.
\subsection{Step 4: Add the Coffee}
Add the ground coffee to the filter and shake it gently to level the grounds.
\subsection{Step 5: Pour the Water}
Start your timer and pour a small amount of hot water over the coffee grounds, just enough to wet them. This is called the "bloom" and allows the coffee to release carbon dioxide, which can affect the taste of the coffee. Wait 30 seconds and then slowly pour the remaining hot water over the coffee in a circular motion, making sure to saturate all the grounds. The total brew time should be around 2-4 minutes.
\subsection{Step 6: Enjoy}
Once the coffee has finished brewing, remove the pour-over coffee maker and discard the used coffee grounds and filter. Pour the coffee into your mug or carafe and enjoy!
\section{Conclusion}
Manual pour-over brewing is a simple and rewarding way to make a delicious cup of coffee. By following these instructions and taking into account the altitude and temperature of your brewing location, you can ensure that your coffee is brewed to perfection every time.

