def linear_search(arr, target):
    for i in range(len(arr)):
        if arr[i] == target:
            return i
    return -1  # target not found
```
This function takes two arguments: `arr` is the array to search, and `target` is the value to look for. It uses a `for` loop to iterate through each element of the array, and checks if the current element is equal to the target value. If it finds a match, it returns the index of that element. If it reaches the end of the array without finding the target, it returns -1 to indicate that the target was not found.
Note that this implementation has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the array. This means that for large arrays, it may not be the most efficient search algorithm. However, it is simple to understand and implement, and works well for small arrays or when the array is unsorted.

