One possible algorithm to sort an array of unique integers in descending order using only bitwise operators in C++ is the Bitonic Sort algorithm. It is a comparison-based sorting algorithm that can be implemented using only bitwise operations.
Here is an example implementation of the Bitonic Sort algorithm in C++:
```
void bitonicSort(int* arr, int n, int up) {
    for (int k = 2; k <= n; k <<= 1) {
        for (int j = k >> 1; j > 0; j >>= 1) {
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                int ij = i ^ j;
                if ((ij) > i == up) {
                    if ((arr[i] ^ arr[ij]) < 0) {
                        int temp = arr[i];
                        arr[i] = arr[ij];
                        arr[ij] = temp;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This implementation takes an array `arr` of length `n`, and a flag `up` indicating whether to sort in ascending or descending order (1 for ascending, 0 for descending). The `bitonicSort` function uses three nested loops to perform the sorting. The outer loop iterates over the length of the array, the middle loop iterates over the bitonic sequence length, and the inner loop iterates over the array elements.
The `ij` variable is used to calculate the index of the element to be compared with the current element `i`. The `if` statement checks whether the two elements should be swapped based on the `up` flag and the sign of the XOR operation between the two elements.
Note that this implementation assumes that the input array contains only positive integers. If the input array may contain negative integers, the comparison in the `if` statement should be modified accordingly.

