In Go, you can use part-of-speech tagging libraries like Go Part-of-Speech (GoPOS) to identify whether a word is a noun or verb. Here's an example code snippet that uses GoPOS to determine the part of speech for the word "Walk":
```
package main
import (
    "fmt"
    "github.com/akshayknarayan/gopos"
)
func main() {
    // Create a new GoPOS tagger
    tagger := gopos.NewTagger("english")
    // Tag the word "Walk"
    tags := tagger.Tag([]string{"Walk"})
    // Check the part of speech for the word "Walk"
    for _, tag := range tags[0] {
        if tag == "VB" {
            fmt.Println("The word Walk is a verb")
        } else if tag == "NN" {
            fmt.Println("The word Walk is a noun")
        } else {
            fmt.Println("The word Walk is neither a verb nor a noun")
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, we first create a new GoPOS tagger for the English language. We then pass the word "Walk" to the tagger's `Tag` method, which returns an array of tags for the input words. We then loop through the tags and check if the word is tagged as a verb ("VB") or a noun ("NN"). If the word is not tagged as either a verb or a noun, we print a message indicating that it is neither.

