```
% Generate RSA keys
p = 61;
q = 53;
n = p*q;
phi = (p-1)*(q-1);
e = 17;
d = modinv(e,phi);
% Encryption
plaintext = 'Hello World';
plaintext_ascii = uint8(plaintext);
ciphertext = modexp(plaintext_ascii,e,n);
% Decryption
decrypted_ascii = modexp(ciphertext,d,n);
decrypted_text = char(decrypted_ascii);
disp(decrypted_text);
```
In this code, we first generate RSA keys by choosing two large prime numbers `p` and `q`. We then calculate `n` as the product of `p` and `q`, and `phi` as the totient of `n`. We choose a public exponent `e` (usually a small prime number such as 17 or 65537), and calculate the private exponent `d` using the modular inverse function `modinv`.
To encrypt a plaintext message, we first convert it to its ASCII representation using the `uint8` function. We then use the modular exponentiation function `modexp` to raise the ASCII values to the power of `e` modulo `n`.
To decrypt the ciphertext, we use the modular exponentiation function again, this time raising the ciphertext values to the power of `d` modulo `n`. We then convert the decrypted ASCII values back to their character representation using the `char` function.
To increase the security of your RSA implementation, you should choose large prime numbers for `p` and `q` (at least 1024 bits each), and ensure that they are truly random. You should also choose a large public exponent `e` (such as 65537), and ensure that it is relatively prime to `phi`. Additionally, you should use padding schemes such as PKCS#1 to prevent attacks such as chosen ciphertext attacks and timing attacks. Finally, you should keep your private key secure and limit its exposure as much as possible.

To calculate the shortest route from Bangkok to Ho Chi Minh City using C++, we can use Dijkstra's algorithm. Here's an implementation:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <queue>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <limits>
using namespace std;
const int INF = numeric_limits<int>::max();
// Define a structure to represent a node in the graph
struct Node {
    int id;
    unordered_map<int, int> neighbors; // Map of neighbor node IDs to edge weights
};
// Define a function to initialize the graph with nodes and edges
vector<Node> initializeGraph() {
    vector<Node> graph(2);
    graph[0].id = 0;
    graph[0].neighbors[1] = 100; // Flight from Bangkok to Ho Chi Minh City
    graph[1].id = 1;
    return graph;
}
// Define a function to calculate the shortest path using Dijkstra's algorithm
vector<int> dijkstra(const vector<Node>& graph, int start, int end) {
    vector<int> distances(graph.size(), INF);
    distances[start] = 0;
    priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>, greater<pair<int, int>>> pq;
    pq.push({0, start});
    while (!pq.empty()) {
        int curr = pq.top().second;
        pq.pop();
        for (const auto& neighbor : graph[curr].neighbors) {
            int neighborId = neighbor.first;
            int edgeWeight = neighbor.second;
            int newDistance = distances[curr] + edgeWeight;
            if (newDistance < distances[neighborId]) {
                distances[neighborId] = newDistance;
                pq.push({newDistance, neighborId});
            }
        }
    }
    // If end node is unreachable, return empty vector
    if (distances[end] == INF) {
        return {};
    }
    // Trace back the shortest path from end to start
    vector<int> path;
    int curr = end;
    while (curr != start) {
        path.push_back(curr);
        for (const auto& neighbor : graph[curr].neighbors) {
            int neighborId = neighbor.first;
            int edgeWeight = neighbor.second;
            if (distances[neighborId] + edgeWeight == distances[curr]) {
                curr = neighborId;
                break;
            }
        }
    }
    path.push_back(start);
    reverse(path.begin(), path.end());
    return path;
}
int main() {
    vector<Node> graph = initializeGraph();
    vector<int> shortestPath = dijkstra(graph, 0, 1);
    // Print the shortest path
    if (shortestPath.empty()) {
        cout << "No path found" << endl;
    } else {
        cout << "Shortest path: ";
        for (int node : shortestPath) {
            cout << node << " ";
        }
        cout << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this implementation, we represent each node in the graph as a `Node` structure, which contains an ID and a map of neighbor node IDs to edge weights. The `initializeGraph` function sets up the graph with two nodes representing Bangkok and Ho Chi Minh City, connected by a flight with a weight of 100.
The `dijkstra` function takes the graph, start node ID, and end node ID as input, and returns the shortest path between the start and end nodes as a vector of node IDs. It uses a priority queue to keep track of the nodes to visit, and a vector of distances to keep track of the shortest distances from the start node to each node. The function also traces back the shortest path from the end node to the start node using the distances vector.
In the `main` function, we call `initializeGraph` to set up the graph, and then call `dijkstra` with the start and end node IDs to calculate the shortest path. We print the shortest path if it exists, or a message indicating that no path was found if it doesn't.

