3. In the JavaScript file, we need to create a function that will draw the leaves on the canvas. We can use the `requestAnimationFrame` method to animate the leaves. For example:
```
function draw() {
  // Clear the canvas
  ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
  // Draw the leaves
  for (var i = 0; i < leaves.length; i++) {
    var leaf = leaves[i];
    // Move the leaf based on wind speed and direction
    leaf.x += leaf.windSpeed;
    leaf.y += leaf.fallSpeed;
    // Rotate the leaf based on wind direction
    ctx.save();
    ctx.translate(leaf.x + leaf.width / 2, leaf.y + leaf.height / 2);
    ctx.rotate(leaf.windDirection * Math.PI / 180);
    ctx.translate(-(leaf.x + leaf.width / 2), -(leaf.y + leaf.height / 2));
    // Draw the leaf
    ctx.drawImage(leaf.image, leaf.x, leaf.y, leaf.width, leaf.height);
    ctx.restore();
    // Reset the leaf position if it goes off the screen
    if (leaf.y > canvas.height) {
      leaf.y = -leaf.height;
      leaf.x = Math.random() * canvas.width;
    }
  }
  // Call the draw function again
  requestAnimationFrame(draw);
}
```
4. In the same JavaScript file, we need to create an array of leaf objects that will be drawn on the canvas. Each leaf object should have properties such as width, height, x position, y position, fall speed, wind speed, and wind direction. We can also preload the leaf image using the `Image` constructor. For example:
