The design pattern that would be best suited for this scenario is the Strategy pattern. The Strategy pattern is used when we have multiple algorithms that can be used to solve a problem, and we want to switch between these algorithms at runtime. In this case, we have multiple algorithms (routes) that can be used to solve the problem (finding the best route), and we want to switch between them depending on the user's preferences.
To implement the Strategy pattern in JavaScript, we can create a RouteStrategy interface with a method called `calculateRoute` that takes two parameters (the starting location and the destination) and returns the best possible route. We can then create different classes that implement this interface and provide their own implementation of the `calculateRoute` method. For example, we can create classes like `FastestRouteStrategy`, `ShortestRouteStrategy`, and `ScenicRouteStrategy`.
Here's an example code snippet:
```
// RouteStrategy interface
class RouteStrategy {
  calculateRoute(start, end) {
    // default implementation
  }
}
// FastestRouteStrategy class
class FastestRouteStrategy extends RouteStrategy {
  calculateRoute(start, end) {
    // implementation to calculate the fastest route
  }
}
// ShortestRouteStrategy class
class ShortestRouteStrategy extends RouteStrategy {
  calculateRoute(start, end) {
    // implementation to calculate the shortest route
  }
}
// ScenicRouteStrategy class
class ScenicRouteStrategy extends RouteStrategy {
  calculateRoute(start, end) {
    // implementation to calculate the most scenic route
  }
}
// RoutePlanner class that uses the RouteStrategy
class RoutePlanner {
  constructor(strategy) {
    this.strategy = strategy;
  }
  setStrategy(strategy) {
    this.strategy = strategy;
  }
  calculateRoute(start, end) {
    return this.strategy.calculateRoute(start, end);
  }
}
// Example usage
const planner = new RoutePlanner(new FastestRouteStrategy());
const route = planner.calculateRoute('New York', 'Los Angeles');
console.log(route); // prints the fastest route from New York to Los Angeles
planner.setStrategy(new ShortestRouteStrategy());
const route2 = planner.calculateRoute('New York', 'Los Angeles');
console.log(route2); // prints the shortest route from New York to Los Angeles
```
In this example, we create different classes that implement the `RouteStrategy` interface and provide their own implementation of the `calculateRoute` method. We then create a `RoutePlanner` class that uses the `RouteStrategy` to calculate the best possible route. We can switch between different strategies at runtime by calling the `setStrategy` method of the `RoutePlanner` class.

