2. Fetch the data from the server: Use an AJAX request to fetch the data from the server. You can use the XMLHttpRequest object or fetch API to make the request.
3. Parse the data: Once you receive the data from the server, parse it into a format that can be used by the charting library.
4. Create the chart: Use the charting library to create the chart. Set the data to the parsed data and customize the chart as per your requirements.
5. Update the chart: To update the chart in real-time, you need to periodically fetch the data from the server and update the chart with the new data. You can use the setInterval function to periodically fetch the data.
Here's an example code snippet that fetches data from a server and updates the chart in real-time using Chart.js:
```javascript
// Step 1: Choose a charting library
var ctx = document.getElementById('myChart').getContext('2d');
var chart = new Chart(ctx, {
    type: 'bar',
    data: {
        labels: [],
        datasets: [{
            label: 'Data',
            data: [],
            backgroundColor: 'rgba(255, 99, 132, 0.2)',
            borderColor: 'rgba(255, 99, 132, 1)',
            borderWidth: 1
        }]
    },
    options: {
        scales: {
            yAxes: [{
                ticks: {
                    beginAtZero: true
                }
            }]
        }
    }
});
// Step 2: Fetch the data from the server
