Certainly! Here is an example of MATLAB code that implements a reinforcement learning agent following the ethical principle of fairness:
```matlab
% Define the matrix of recipients
recipients = [1 2 3; 4 5 6; 7 8 9];
% Define the vector of resources
resources = [10 10 10];
% Define the number of episodes
num_episodes = 100;
% Define the learning rate
alpha = 0.1;
% Define the discount factor
gamma = 0.9;
% Define the exploration rate
epsilon = 0.1;
% Initialize the Q-value matrix
Q = zeros(size(recipients));
% Loop over the episodes
for episode = 1:num_episodes
    % Reset the state to the initial state
    state = recipients;

    % Loop over the steps in the episode
    while true
        % Choose an action using an epsilon-greedy policy
        if rand() < epsilon
            % Choose a random action
            action = randi(length(resources));
        else
            % Choose the action with the highest Q-value
            [~, action] = max(Q(state));
        end

        % Update the state and the resources based on the action
        [new_state, reward] = allocate_resources(state, action, resources);

        % Update the Q-value for the previous state and action
        Q(state)(action) = Q(state)(action) + alpha * (reward + gamma * max(Q(new_state)) - Q(state)(action));

        % Update the state and the resources
        state = new_state;
        resources = resources - state(action, :);

        % Check if the episode is over
        if all(resources <= 0)
            break;
        end
    end
end
% Define a function to allocate resources to recipients
function [new_state, reward] = allocate_resources(state, action, resources)
    % Allocate the resources to the recipients based on the action
    allocation = zeros(size(state));
    allocation(action, :) = resources(action) / size(state, 2);
    new_state = state + allocation;

    % Calculate the reward based on the ethical principle of fairness
    fairness = max(new_state(:)) / min(new_state(:));
    reward = log(fairness);
end
```
In this code, we first define the matrix of recipients and the vector of resources. We also define the number of episodes, learning rate, discount factor, and exploration rate for the reinforcement learning algorithm. We then initialize the Q-value matrix to zeros.
We then loop over the episodes and within each episode, we loop over the steps. At each step, we choose an action using an epsilon-greedy policy, update the Q-value for the previous state and action, and allocate the resources to the recipients based on the action. We also calculate the reward based on the ethical principle of fairness, which is defined as the ratio of the maximum allocation to the minimum allocation. We use the natural logarithm of this ratio as the reward.
Finally, we define a function `allocate_resources` that takes the current state, the chosen action, and the vector of resources as inputs, and returns the new state and the reward. The function allocates the resources to the recipients based on the action, and calculates the reward based on the ethical principle of fairness.
Note that this is just one possible implementation of a reinforcement learning agent following an ethical principle. The specific ethical principle and the simulation environment can be customized based on the specific application.

