\textbf{Introduction:}
Sonnet 18, written by William Shakespeare, is one of the most famous and beloved sonnets of all time. The poem is written in the form of a love poem, in which the speaker compares his beloved to a summer's day. However, he quickly realizes that his beloved is far more beautiful and temperate than any summer's day could ever be. The sonnet goes on to explore the idea of the impermanence of beauty and the power of poetry to preserve that beauty for eternity.
\textbf{Literary Devices:}
Shakespeare uses a number of literary devices in this sonnet to convey his message. One of the most prominent devices is the use of metaphor. The speaker compares his beloved to a summer's day, but quickly realizes that she is far more beautiful and temperate than any summer's day could ever be. This metaphor is then extended throughout the poem, as the speaker explores the impermanence of beauty and the power of poetry to preserve it.
Another device used by Shakespeare is personification. He personifies the eye of heaven, giving it the ability to shine too hot or to have its gold complexion dimmed. This personification emphasizes the idea that even nature is subject to change and impermanence.
Shakespeare also uses imagery to convey his message. He uses the image of the rough winds shaking the darling buds of May to illustrate the idea of impermanence. Similarly, the image of every fair declining emphasizes the idea that beauty is fleeting and subject to change.
\textbf{Theme:}
The theme of Sonnet 18 is the power of poetry to preserve beauty for eternity. The speaker realizes that his beloved is far more beautiful and temperate than any summer's day could ever be. However, he also realizes that beauty is fleeting and subject to change. The speaker then turns to poetry as a way to preserve his beloved's beauty for eternity. He argues that as long as men can breathe and eyes can see, his beloved's beauty will live on through his poetry.
\textbf{Overall Meaning:}
The overall meaning of Sonnet 18 is that beauty is impermanent, but poetry has the power to preserve it for eternity. The speaker compares his beloved to a summer's day, but quickly realizes that she is far more beautiful and temperate than any summer's day could ever be. However, he also realizes that beauty is fleeting and subject to change. The speaker turns to poetry as a way to preserve his beloved's beauty for eternity. He argues that as long as men can breathe and eyes can see, his beloved's beauty will live on through his poetry. The sonnet is a celebration of the power of love and the power of poetry to transcend time and preserve beauty for eternity.
