def find_sum_indices(arr, target):
    def divide_conquer(start, end):
        if start == end:
            return []
        mid = (start + end) // 2
        left_indices = divide_conquer(start, mid)
        right_indices = divide_conquer(mid + 1, end)
        left_sum_set = set([0])
        left_sum = 0
        for i in range(mid, start - 1, -1):
            left_sum += arr[i]
            left_sum_set.add(left_sum)
        right_sum = 0
        for i in range(mid + 1, end + 1):
            right_sum += arr[i]
            if target - right_sum in left_sum_set:
                for j in range(len(left_indices)):
                    if left_indices[j] >= start and left_indices[j] <= mid and left_sum - arr[left_indices[j]] == target - right_sum:
                        return left_indices[:j] + [i] + left_indices[j:]
                left_indices.append(i)
        return left_indices + right_indices

    return divide_conquer(0, len(arr) - 1)
```
The function takes in an array `arr` and a target value `target`, and returns a list of indices of elements that sum to the target value. The implementation uses a divide and conquer algorithm with a time complexity of O(nlogn) or less.
The `divide_conquer` function is a recursive helper function that takes in the start and end indices of the subarray it is currently working on. It first checks if the subarray has only one element, in which case it returns an empty list. Otherwise, it splits the subarray into two halves and recursively calls itself on each half.
For each half, it computes the set of all possible sums of elements in that half. It then iterates through the elements in the other half, computing the sum of those elements and checking if the target value minus that sum is in the set of possible sums for the first half. If it is, it searches through the list of indices returned by the recursive call on the first half to find an index that matches the sum required to reach the target value. If it finds one, it returns the list of indices with the current index inserted at the appropriate position. If it doesn't find one, it appends the current index to the list of indices for the first half.
Finally, it returns the concatenation of the lists of indices for each half.
The main `find_sum_indices` function simply calls `divide_conquer` on the entire array and returns the resulting list of indices.

