\textbf{Tornado Formation}
A tornado is a rapidly rotating column of air that is in contact with both the surface of the Earth and a cumulonimbus cloud. It is formed when there is a strong updraft of warm, moist air and a downdraft of cold, dry air. This causes a horizontal rotation to form in the atmosphere, which is then tilted vertically by the updraft. The rotation becomes stronger and more concentrated as it is stretched vertically, creating a funnel-shaped cloud. As the funnel cloud descends, it can pick up debris and dust from the ground, making it visible.
The equation for the formation of a tornado can be written as:
\begin{equation}
Tornado = (Updraft + Downdraft) \times Rotation
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
\item Updraft = a vertical movement of warm, moist air
\item Downdraft = a vertical movement of cold, dry air
\item Rotation = a horizontal rotation in the atmosphere
\end{itemize}
In summary, a tornado is formed by the combination of an updraft and downdraft of air, along with a horizontal rotation in the atmosphere. This creates a funnel-shaped cloud that can be dangerous and destructive.

