Challenge accepted! Here are five different ways to reverse a string in Swift, each with a different time and space complexity:
1. Using the built-in `reversed()` method:
```swift
func reverseString1(_ s: String) -> String {
    return String(s.reversed())
}
```
Time complexity: O(n), where n is the length of the string. This is because the `reversed()` method iterates over the characters in the string once.
Space complexity: O(n), because a new string is created to hold the reversed characters.
2. Using a loop to iterate over the characters in the string and append them to a new string in reverse order:
```swift
func reverseString2(_ s: String) -> String {
    var reversed = ""
    for char in s {
        reversed = "\(char)\(reversed)"
    }
    return reversed
}
```
Time complexity: O(n^2), because each time a character is appended to the new string, all the existing characters have to be shifted over by one position.
Space complexity: O(n), because a new string is created to hold the reversed characters.
3. Using a loop to iterate over the characters in the string and swap the first and last characters, then move inwards until the whole string is reversed:
```swift
func reverseString3(_ s: String) -> String {
    var chars = Array(s)
    var left = 0
    var right = s.count - 1
    while left < right {
        let temp = chars[left]
        chars[left] = chars[right]
        chars[right] = temp
        left += 1
        right -= 1
    }
    return String(chars)
}
```
Time complexity: O(n), because each character is swapped once.
Space complexity: O(n), because an array of characters is created to hold the reversed characters.
4. Using recursion to reverse the substring of the string starting from the second character, then appending the first character to the end:
```swift
func reverseString4(_ s: String) -> String {
    if s.count <= 1 {
        return s
    }
    let index = s.index(s.startIndex, offsetBy: 1)
    let substring = String(s[index...])
    return reverseString4(substring) + String(s.first!)
}
```
Time complexity: O(n^2), because each recursive call creates a new substring that is one character shorter than the previous one.
Space complexity: O(n^2), because each recursive call creates a new substring that is one character shorter than the previous one.
5. Using a stack to push each character onto the stack, then pop them off one by one to create the reversed string:
```swift
func reverseString5(_ s: String) -> String {
    var stack = [Character]()
    for char in s {
        stack.append(char)
    }
    var reversed = ""
    while !stack.isEmpty {
        reversed.append(stack.popLast()!)
    }
    return reversed
}
```
Time complexity: O(n), because each character is pushed onto the stack once, and popped off once.
Space complexity: O(n), because a stack is created to hold the characters in the string. 
So the order of these approaches in ascending order of time complexity is:
2. O(n^2)
4. O(n^2)
1. O(n)
3. O(n)
5. O(n)

