Yes, here's a Java code that can identify independent and dependent clauses in a given sentence:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;
public class ClauseIdentifier {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter a sentence: ");
        String sentence = scanner.nextLine();
        String[] clauses = sentence.split(", |\\s+(?=(?:that|which|who|whom|whose))");
        if (clauses.length == 1) {
            System.out.println("The sentence is a simple sentence.");
            System.out.println("Independent clause: " + clauses[0]);
        } else if (clauses.length == 2) {
            System.out.println("The sentence is a complex sentence.");
            System.out.println("Dependent clause: " + clauses[0]);
            System.out.println("Independent clause: " + clauses[1]);
        } else {
            System.out.println("The sentence is a compound-complex sentence.");
            System.out.println("Dependent clause 1: " + clauses[0]);
            System.out.println("Independent clause: " + clauses[1]);
            System.out.println("Dependent clause 2: " + clauses[2]);
        }
    }
}
```
Here's how the code works:
- It prompts the user to enter a sentence.
- It splits the sentence into clauses based on commas and relative pronouns (that, which, who, whom, whose) using a regular expression.
- It checks the number of clauses and identifies the type of sentence.
- It prints the dependent and independent clauses accordingly.
Note that this code assumes that the sentence contains at most one dependent clause. If the sentence contains more than one dependent clause, the code will only identify the first dependent clause and treat the rest as part of the independent clause.

