```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;
vector<int> sieveOfEratosthenes(int n) {
    vector<bool> isPrime(n+1, true);
    vector<int> primes;
    for (int i = 2; i*i <= n; i++) {
        if (isPrime[i]) {
            for (int j = i*i; j <= n; j += i) {
                isPrime[j] = false;
            }
        }
    }
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        if (isPrime[i]) {
            primes.push_back(i);
        }
    }
    return primes;
}
int main() {
    int n = 100;
    vector<int> primes = sieveOfEratosthenes(n);
    cout << "Prime numbers between 1 and " << n << " are: ";
    for (int i = 0; i < primes.size(); i++) {
        cout << primes[i] << " ";
    }
    return 0;
}
```
The time complexity of the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm is O(n log log n), where n is the upper limit of the range of numbers to check for primes. The algorithm works by creating a boolean array of size n+1, where each element is initially set to true. The algorithm then iterates over all numbers from 2 to the square root of n, and for each prime number found, it marks all its multiples as composite by setting their corresponding boolean values to false. Finally, all the remaining numbers with boolean value true are prime numbers and are returned in a vector.
In the above code, the `sieveOfEratosthenes()` function takes an integer n as input and returns a vector of all prime numbers between 1 and n. The function first creates a boolean vector `isPrime` of size n+1, where each element is initially set to true. It then iterates over all numbers from 2 to the square root of n, and for each prime number found, it marks all its multiples as composite by setting their corresponding boolean values to false. Finally, all the remaining numbers with boolean value true are prime numbers and are added to a vector `primes`, which is then returned.
