\textbf{Analysis of Helena's Character in "A Midsummer Night's Dream"}
\textbf{Loyalty and Perseverance}
Helena is a character in "A Midsummer Night's Dream" who demonstrates remarkable loyalty and perseverance in her pursuit of Demetrius. Despite his rejection of her, Helena remains devoted to him, even going so far as to follow him into the forest. She says, "I will go tell him of fair Hermia's flight. Then to the wood will he tomorrow night/Pursue her; and for this intelligence/If I have thanks, it is a dear expense" (Act I, Scene 1). This shows that she is willing to risk her own safety and well-being to help Demetrius, even though he does not reciprocate her feelings. 
Furthermore, Helena's loyalty is also evident in her willingness to sacrifice her own happiness for the sake of Demetrius. She says, "And even for that do I love you the more. I am your spaniel; and, Demetrius, The more you beat me, I will fawn on you" (Act II, Scene 1). This quote shows that Helena is willing to endure mistreatment from Demetrius if it means being close to him. 
\textbf{Love for Demetrius}
Helena's love for Demetrius is the driving force behind her actions and relationships with other characters. She is willing to risk everything to win his love, including her friendship with Hermia. When Hermia accuses her of stealing Demetrius's love, Helena responds, "I love thee not, therefore pursue me not/Where is Lysander and fair Hermia? The one I'll slay, the other slayeth me" (Act II, Scene 2). This quote shows that Helena is willing to sacrifice her friendship with Hermia in order to win Demetrius's love. 
Moreover, Helena's love for Demetrius also causes her to become jealous and irrational. When Demetrius tells her to leave him alone, she responds, "I am sick when I do look on thee" (Act II, Scene 1). This quote shows that Helena's love for Demetrius has become an obsession that is causing her emotional pain. 
\textbf{Contribution to Themes}
Helena's character contributes to the themes of the play in several ways. Her loyalty and perseverance demonstrate the power of love and its ability to motivate people to do extraordinary things. Additionally, her love for Demetrius challenges traditional gender roles and societal expectations. In the play, women are expected to be passive and obedient, but Helena defies these expectations by actively pursuing Demetrius. 
Furthermore, Helena's character also highlights the theme of the fluidity of love. At the beginning of the play, she is in love with Demetrius, but by the end, she is in love with Lysander. This shows that love is not fixed and can change depending on the circumstances. 
In conclusion, Helena is a complex character in "A Midsummer Night's Dream" who demonstrates loyalty, perseverance, and a willingness to challenge societal expectations. Her love for Demetrius drives her actions and relationships with other characters, contributing to the themes of the play.

