The Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm is an efficient way to generate prime numbers. It works by creating a boolean array of size `n+1`, where each element is initialized to `true`. Starting from 2, it marks all multiples of 2 as `false`. Then it moves to the next unmarked number (3) and marks all its multiples as `false`. It continues this process until all numbers up to `n` have been considered. The remaining unmarked numbers are prime.
Here's an example of how to use the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm to generate prime numbers in Java:
```java
public static void generatePrimes(int n) {
    boolean[] isPrime = new boolean[n+1];
    Arrays.fill(isPrime, true);
    for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(n); i++) {
        if (isPrime[i]) {
            for (int j = i*i; j <= n; j += i) {
                isPrime[j] = false;
            }
        }
    }
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        if (isPrime[i]) {
            System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
    }
}
```
