The Battle of Waterloo was fought on June 18, 1815, near the town of Waterloo in present-day Belgium. It was a decisive battle that ended the Napoleonic Wars, which had been raging in Europe for over a decade. The battle was fought between the French army, led by Emperor Napoleon Bonaparte, and a coalition of forces from the United Kingdom, the Netherlands, and Prussia, led by the Duke of Wellington and Field Marshal Blücher. The battle is considered one of the most significant in European history, as it marked the end of Napoleon's reign and the beginning of a new era in Europe.
\section{Overview of the armies and their leaders}
The French army was composed of approximately 72,000 soldiers, while the coalition forces had a combined strength of around 118,000 soldiers. Napoleon was the commander of the French army, while the coalition forces were led by the Duke of Wellington and Field Marshal Blücher.
\section{Events leading up to and during the battle}
Napoleon had been exiled to the island of Elba in 1814 after his defeat in the War of the Sixth Coalition. However, he escaped and returned to France in March 1815, and quickly regained power. The coalition forces mobilized to stop him, and the two sides met at Waterloo.
The battle began with the French army attacking the coalition forces. However, the coalition forces were able to hold their ground and repel the French attacks. The battle continued throughout the day, with both sides suffering heavy casualties. In the late afternoon, the Prussian army arrived to reinforce the coalition forces, and this turned the tide of the battle in their favor. The French army was eventually defeated, and Napoleon was forced to abdicate once again.
